We are in the process of recruiting staff to support a newly funded
project at Northwestern University (Chicago) and the University of New
Mexico (Albuquerque).  The project will build a new open source
virtual machine monitor for modern architectures that we intend will
be useful for research in computer systems, computer architecture, and
high performance computing, and in teaching.   A central idea is to
use compile-time and run-time composition to be able to produce VMMs
specialized for different roles from the same compact codebase.

We are expressly interested in individuals that have the following
attributes:

- Demonstrated ability to do effective kernel development, or low-
level embedded systems development
- Demonstrated ability to both work individually and in small teams
- Familiarity with IA32 (64 bit extensions and VT or SVM familiarity a
plus)
- Affinity for hacking
- Desire to be in an academic environment
- Desire to become an integral part of research groups working in
systems, architecture, and HPC
- Desire to work on an open-source project and interact with the
broader open-source development community

We are willing to consider candidates at all degree levels and are
willing to be flexible - we mainly care about demonstrated skills and
evidence of the ability to follow through.     Positions will be for
2-3 years, with the possibility of extension.   We intend to hire
individuals as project scientists, and may also be able to provide
joint project scientist / graduate student positions for qualified
candidates.

If you are interested, please contact us at r e c r u i t i n g @ p r
e s c i e n c e l a b . o r  g (remove spaces), including a resume or
CV.

Peter Dinda, Northwestern University
Arthur Maccabe, University of New Mexico
Fabian Bustamante, Northwestern University
Russell Joseph, Northwestern University

